# Cloudflare Pages headers.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE DEPLOYING A WEBGL BUILD:
# Cloudflare Pages rejects any single file over 25 MiB (and 20,000 files per
# deployment on the free plan). Unity WebGL emits ONE Build.data and ONE
# Build.wasm, both of which pass 25 MiB for anything beyond a trivial scene, so a
# naive `wrangler pages deploy` of a real build fails outright.
#
# The working layout is:
#   - Pages hosts index.html and Build/*.loader.js only (small).
#   - R2 (cdn.<your-domain>) hosts Build/*.data.br, *.wasm.br, *.framework.js.br,
#     which costs $0 in egress and has no per-file limit worth worrying about.
#   - Point the loader's config at the cdn URLs, and set the Content-Encoding
#     headers below on the R2 objects instead (R2 stores them as object metadata,
#     set with `wrangler r2 object put --content-encoding br`).
#
# The rules below still apply to whatever Build/* files remain on Pages, and are
# the reference for what the R2 object metadata has to say.
#
# THE UNITY WEBGL GOTCHA, WRITTEN DOWN SO YOU ONLY LOSE AN EVENING TO IT ONCE:
# Unity ships its WebGL build pre-compressed as .br (Brotli) or .gz (gzip) and
# expects the server to hand those bytes over with a Content-Encoding header so
# the browser inflates them. Cloudflare Pages will not infer that from the file
# extension. Without the rules below the browser downloads a Brotli blob, tries
# to parse it as WebAssembly, and you get "Unable to parse Build/x.wasm" or a
# silent black canvas.
#
# In Unity: Project Settings > Player > WebGL > Publishing Settings
#   Compression Format:      Brotli
#   Decompression Fallback:  off  (leave on only if you must serve from a host
#                                  you cannot set headers on - it costs ~100KB
#                                  of JS and a slower load)

/Build/*.br
  Content-Encoding: br
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/Build/*.wasm.br
  Content-Type: application/wasm
  Content-Encoding: br

/Build/*.js.br
  Content-Type: application/javascript
  Content-Encoding: br

/Build/*.data.br
  Content-Type: application/octet-stream
  Content-Encoding: br

/Build/*.gz
  Content-Encoding: gzip
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/Build/*.wasm.gz
  Content-Type: application/wasm
  Content-Encoding: gzip

/Build/*.js.gz
  Content-Type: application/javascript
  Content-Encoding: gzip

/Build/*.data.gz
  Content-Type: application/octet-stream
  Content-Encoding: gzip

# Unity 6 can use SharedArrayBuffer for threaded builds, which browsers only
# expose to cross-origin-isolated pages. Harmless if you never enable threads.
/play/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp

# Baseline hardening for the rest of the site.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()

# Separately, and more importantly than any header: a GTA-scale open world is not
# a realistic WebGL target. Browsers cap out around 2-4 GB of heap and there is
# no streaming install. Anything served here has to be a deliberately small
# sliced-off scene - a shooting range, one building - not the game.
